    "created_at": "2017-10-04T23:54:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-04T23:54:50Z",
    "body": "Animated images are a bit awkward to work with in this case. I think we agree that if the animation hasn't started, then we mean the first frame should be decoded so we can insert it and start the animation without jank.\r\n\r\nWhen it comes to an animated images in the middle of an animation, the current implementation in Chrome decodes the _current_ frame and keeps that available. The wording in the spec does seem to suggest that it should be the first frame, but I read that paragraph as just an example. It can probably be worded better.\r\n\r\nThe intent of the spec is that when the promise resolves, and the image is inserted, whatever frame the browser needs to rasterize is ready.\r\n\r\nAs a counterpoint to the proposal, suppose each frame of the GIF is 100ms (ie it moves at 10 frames per second). If we display frame_1 and immediately call decode on it, then it will resolve the promise when the frame_2 is done. Suppose that's quick (<1ms). What are the actions that the developer can do to take advantage of this? They can't force the animation to advance any faster than the browser would do it. They also can't guarantee that the frame_2 will still be available when the browser does advance it, since the spec guarantees availability for basically the next raf only.\r\n\r\nI'm not necessarily opposed to the spirit of the proposal, I'm just trying to understand the use cases. Also to reiterate, I don't think the main use of this api would be to deal with already animating images, since the set of possible and reasonable actions to take in the promise resolution seems unclear. ",
